    "content": "{\n  \"search_id\": \"search_0f04f619736091de61c9aa0c8b8a9072\",\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.cell.com/cell-reports/pdf/S2211-1247(25)01190-8.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"AAVs targeting human carbonic anhydrase IV enhance gene ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Report\\nAAVs targeting human carbonic anhydrase IV\\nenhance gene delivery to the brain\\nGraphical abstract\\nHighlights\\n• Receptor-targeted AAV engineering and selection via hybrid\\nin vitro / in vivo pipeline\\n• Human receptor expression in mice allows rapid assessment\\nof translational AAVs\\n• Two AAVs targeting human CA-IV potently cross a\\nhumanized blood-brain barrier in vivo\\nAuthors\\nChangfan Lin, Xinhong Chen,\\nJonathan D. Hoang, ..., Andrew D. Steele,\\nTimothy F. Shay, Viviana Gradinaru\\nCorrespondence\\nviviana@caltech.edu\\nIn brief\\nAdeno-associated viral vectors (AAVs)\\nbased on natural serotypes poorly\\ntransduce the brain after intravenous\\nadministration, limiting clinical efficacy.\\nLin et al. engineer AAV9 to engage the\\nhuman version of CA-IV, a blood-brain\\nbarrier receptor used previously by\\npotent but mouse-specific vectors, and\\nvalidate their function in a ‘‘humanized’’\\nmouse model.\\nLin et al., 2025, Cell Reports 44 , 116419\\nNovember 25, 2025 © 2025 The Author(s). Published by Elsevier Inc.\\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.celrep.2025.116419\\nll\\nReport\\nAAVs targeting human carbonic anhydrase IV\\nenhance gene delivery to the brain\\nChangfan Lin, 1 Xinhong Chen, 1 Jonathan D. Hoang, 1 Fiona Ristic, 1 Yujie Fan, 1 Seongmin Jang, 1 Jin Hyung Alex Chung, 1\\nErin E. Sullivan, 1 Tomasz Gawda, 1 Bill Kavvathas, 1 Irene Tran, 2 Yitong Li, 1 Andrew D.\\nbrain-enhanced engineered AAV capsids for various model organisms, but inter-species differences chal-\\nlenge their translation. Here, we engineer AAVs to target human carbonic anhydrase IV (CA-IV), a recently\\nidentified blood-brain barrier transcytosis receptor with several favorable properties. We perform in vitro\\ntop-ranked in vitro candidates. One of these, AAV-hCA4-IV77, achieves 100-fold-greater brain transduction\\nthan AAV9, with robust neuronal and astrocytic coverage across brain regions. These results advance our\\nunderstanding of receptor-targeted capsid design and support the therapeutic potential of human CA-IV-\\nengaging AAVs.\\nINTRODUCTION\\nfied the BBB receptors utilized by certain engineered systemic\\ncapsids to cross into the brain through receptor-mediated trans-\\ncytosis. 32–36 One such receptor is carbonic anhydrase IV (CA-\\nIV), which facilitates the efficient BBB crossing of AAV variants\\n9P31 and 9P36 in mice. 18 , 32 Importantly, CA-IV is also present\\nhCA4-IV68 and AAV-hCA4-IV77 demonstrate significantly enhanced and widespread transduction throughout the brains of ‘‘humanized’’ mice compared to\\ncontrol mice, while AAV9 shows consistently poor brain transduction in both groups. Scale bars, 1,000 μ m.\\nCell Reports 44 , 116419, November 25, 2025\\n7\\nReport\\nll\\nOPEN ACCESS\\n(legend on next page)\\n8\\nCell Reports 44 , 116419, November 25, 2025\\nReport\\nll\\nOPEN ACCESS\\nRESOURCE AVAILABILITY\\nLead contact\\nFurther information and requests for resources and reagents should be\\ndirected to and will be fulfilled by the lead contact, Viviana Gradinaru\\n( viviana@caltech.edu ).\\nMaterials availability\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.cell.com/cell-reports/fulltext/S2211-1247(25)01190-8\",\n      \"title\": \"AAVs targeting human carbonic anhydrase IV enhance gene delivery to the brain: Cell Reports\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2025-11-25\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"* Submit\\n* Log in\\n* Log in\\n* Get Institutional Access\\n* Register\\n* open links dialog\\n  Access provided by\\n  Report Volume 44 , Issue 11 116419 November 25, 2025 Open access\\nAAVs targeting human carbonic anhydrase IV enhance gene delivery to the brain\\nHighlights\\n•\\nReceptor-targeted AAV engineering and selection via hybrid in vitro / in vivo pipeline\\n•\\nHuman receptor expression in mice allows rapid assessment of translational AAVs\\n•\\nTwo AAVs targeting human CA-IV potently cross a humanized blood-brain barrier in vivo\\nSummary\\nHere, we engineer AAVs to target human carbonic anhydrase IV (CA-IV), a recently identified blood-brain barrier transcytosis receptor with several favorable properties. We perform in vitro on-column selection of an AAV library to exclude capsids that do not target human CA-IV.\\nWe subsequently screen the top 0.01% of variants in vivo in mice expressing human CA-IV in brain endothelial cells. Notably, two top-performing capsids in “humanized” mice are relatively lower ranked in vitro and outperform several top-ranked in vitro candidates.\\nOne of these, AAV-hCA4-IV77, achieves 100-fold-greater brain transduction than AAV9, with robust neuronal and astrocytic coverage across brain regions. These results advance our understanding of receptor-targeted capsid design and support the therapeutic potential of human CA-IV-engaging AAVs.\\n32 , 33 , 34 , 35 , 36 One such receptor is carbonic anhydrase IV (CA-IV), which facilitates the efficient BBB crossing of AAV variants 9P31 and 9P36 in mice.\\nAfter narrowing down the pool of engineered AAVs in vitro , single capsid variants were characterized in vivo in a mouse model expressing human CA-IV in brain endothelial cells.\\nThese results inform future capsid engineering pipelines and support the translational potential of AAVs targeted to human CA-IV.\\nShow full caption\\n(A) Results of transcytosis-based selection in “humanized” mice expressing human CA-IV in brain endothelial cells. Identified variants AAV-hCA4-IV68 and AAV-hCA4-IV77 show high Eh and Er scores, while previously identified top in vitro performers (Alpha 41, 48, 49, and 62) demonstrate poor performance in this in vivo context.\\n(C) Comparison of AAV performance in round 2 cell-transduction-based selection versus in vivo transcytosis selection. The top-performing variants in “humanized” mice (AAV-hCA4-IV68, AAV-hCA4-IV77, and light green colored variants) are highlighted.\\nBoth AAV-hCA4-IV68 and AAV-hCA4-IV77 demonstrate significantly enhanced and widespread transduction throughout the brains of “humanized” mice compared to control mice, while AAV9 shows consistently poor brain transduction in both groups. Scale bars, 1,000 μm.\\n18 Among the top 0.01% on-column performers, in vivo selection revealed two variants, AAV-hCA4-IV68 and AAV-hCA4-IV77 (hereafter abbreviated as IV68 and IV77), which outperformed several candidate vectors identified previously through fully in vitro selection (Alpha 48, 49, and 62) (Figure 3A).\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41175373\",\n      \"title\": \"AAVs targeting human carbonic anhydrase IV enhance gene delivery to the brain - PubMed\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2025-11-25\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Here, we engineer AAVs to target human carbonic anhydrase IV (CA-IV), a recently identified blood-brain barrier transcytosis receptor with several favorable properties. We perform in vitro on-column selection of an AAV library to exclude capsids that do not target human CA-IV.\\nWe subsequently screen the top 0.01% of variants in vivo in mice expressing human CA-IV in brain endothelial cells. Notably, two top-performing capsids in \\\"humanized\\\" mice are relatively lower ranked in vitro and outperform several top-ranked in vitro candidates.\\nOne of these, AAV-hCA4-IV77, achieves 100-fold-greater brain transduction than AAV9, with robust neuronal and astrocytic coverage across brain regions. These results advance our understanding of receptor-targeted capsid design and support the therapeutic potential of human CA-IV-engaging AAVs.\\nKeywords: AAV; CNS; CP: Molecular biology; CP: Neuroscience; blood-brain barrier; gene therapy.\\nCopyright © 2025 The Author(s). Published by Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.\\nPubMed Disclaimer\\n(B) Comparison of AAV performance in round 2 pull-down-based selection versus in vivo transcytosis selection. Scores for each variant were averaged across mice ( n = 3). Notably, the top-performing variants in “humanized” mice (AAV-hCA4-IV68, AAV-hCA4-IV77, and light green colored variants) were not ranked highly in the pull-down selection.\\n(C) Comparison of AAV performance in round 2 cell-transduction-based selection versus in vivo transcytosis selection. The top-performing variants in “humanized” mice (AAV-hCA4-IV68, AAV-hCA4-IV77, and light green colored variants) are highlighted.\\n(D) Representative sagittal brain sections showing transduction by AAV9, AAV-hCA4-IV68, and AAV-hCA4-IV77 in control mice versus “humanized” mice expressing human CA-IV. To prevent saturation of the fluorescence signal in brain sections, images were captured using reduced illumination intensity.\\nBoth AAV-hCA4-IV68 and AAV-hCA4-IV77 demonstrate significantly enhanced and widespread transduction throughout the brains of “humanized” mice compared to control mice, while AAV9 shows consistently poor brain transduction in both groups. Scale bars, 1,000 μm.\\nFigure 4.. Validation in a “humanized” mouse…\\nFigure 4..\\nAAV-hCA4-IV77 (blue circles) and AAV-hCA4-IV68 (orange circles) show a positive trend with increased transcript levels in organs expressing higher amounts of CA-IV, while AAV9 (gray circles) shows no correlation with CA-IV expression. (E) Representative images of human brain organoid transduction comparing AAV9, AAV-hCA4-IV77, and AAV-hCA4-IV68.\\n10.1038/nrd.2018.158. - DOI - PubMed\\n6. Challis RC, Ravindra Kumar S, Chen X, Goertsen D, Coughlin GM, Hori AM, Chuapoco MR, Otis TS, Miles TF, and Gradinaru V (2022). Adeno-Associated Virus Toolkit to Target Diverse Brain Cells. Annu. Rev. Neurosci. 45, 447–469. 10.1146/annurevneuro-111020-100834. - DOI - PubMed\\n8.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12942408\",\n      \"title\": \"\\n            AAVs targeting human carbonic anhydrase IV enhance gene delivery to the brain - PMC\\n        \",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"AAVs targeting human carbonic anhydrase IV enhance gene delivery to the brain\\nHere, we engineer AAVs to target human carbonic anhydrase IV (CA-IV), a recently identified blood-brain barrier transcytosis receptor with several favorable properties. We perform in vitro on-column selection of an AAV library to exclude capsids that do not target human CA-IV.\\nWe subsequently screen the top 0.01% of variants in vivo in mice expressing human CA-IV in brain endothelial cells. Notably, two top-performing capsids in “humanized” mice are relatively lower ranked in vitro and outperform several top-ranked in vitro candidates.\\nOne of these, AAV-hCA4-IV77, achieves 100-fold-greater brain transduction than AAV9, with robust neuronal and astrocytic coverage across brain regions. These results advance our understanding of receptor-targeted capsid design and support the therapeutic potential of human CA-IV-engaging AAVs.\\nengineer AAV9 to engage the human version of CA-IV, a blood-brain barrier receptor used previously by potent but mouse-specific vectors, and validate their function in a “humanized” mouse model.\\nThus, a different approach for engineering human-optimized systemic AAVs is needed.\\nThe central nervous system (CNS) is an especially challenging target for gene therapy due to the blood-brain barrier (BBB).\\n32 – 36 One such receptor is carbonic anhydrase IV (CA-IV), which facilitates the efficient BBB crossing of AAV variants 9P31 and 9P36 in mice.\\nAfter narrowing down the pool of engineered AAVs in vitro , single capsid variants were characterized in vivo in a mouse model expressing human CA-IV in brain endothelial cells.\\n(D) Representative sagittal brain sections showing transduction by AAV9, AAV-hCA4-IV68, and AAV-hCA4-IV77 in control mice versus “humanized” mice expressing human CA-IV. To prevent saturation of the fluorescence signal in brain sections, images were captured using reduced illumination intensity.\\nBoth AAV-hCA4-IV68 and AAV-hCA4-IV77 demonstrate significantly enhanced and widespread transduction throughout the brains of “humanized” mice compared to control mice, while AAV9 shows consistently poor brain transduction in both groups. Scale bars, 1,000 μm.\\nWe also individually confirmed that identified variant capsids internalize into and transduce HEK293 cells in a human CA-IV-dependent manner.\\n18 Among the top 0.01% on-column performers, in vivo selection revealed two variants, AAV-hCA4-IV68 and AAV-hCA4-IV77 (hereafter abbreviated as IV68 and IV77), which outperformed several candidate vectors identified previously through fully in vitro selection (Alpha 48, 49, and 62) ( Figure 3A ).\\nIntegrating rapid in vitro engineering and “humanized” in vivo selections, we successfully identified human CA-IV-specific capsid variants capable of efficient transcytosis, with IV77 demonstrating a 100-fold increase in brain transduction efficiency compared to AAV9 in “humanized” mice and a 250-fold increase compared to control mice.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2025.04.21.649868v1.full\",\n      \"title\": \"AAVs Targeting Human Carbonic Anhydrase IV Enhance Gene Delivery to the Brain | bioRxiv\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2025-01-01\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Steele , View ORCID Profile Timothy F. Shay , View ORCID Profile Viviana Gradinaru\\ndoi: https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.04.21.649868\\nHere, we engineer AAVs to target human carbonic anhydrase IV (CA-IV), a recently identified blood-brain barrier (BBB) transcytosis receptor. Among known transcytosis receptors, CA-IV is notable for its relatively specific expression in brain endothelial cells and the potency of AAVs that target it in mice.\\nWe first used in vitro receptor-based selection of a vast AAV library to exclude capsids that do not bind human CA-IV, followed by in vivo selection in “humanized” mice expressing human CA-IV in brain endothelial cells.\\nNotably, we find that human CA-IV binding capsid variants that were poorly enriched in the pull-down selection outperform strong binders in vivo. The most promising vector, AAV-hCA4-IV77, engages human CA-IV to achieve 100-fold greater brain transduction than AAV9, with robust neuronal and astrocytic coverage throughout multiple brain regions.\\nThus, a different approach for engineering human-optimized systemic AAVs is needed.\\nThe central nervous system is an especially challenging target for gene therapy due to the blood-brain barrier (BBB).\\nOne such receptor is carbonic anhydrase IV (CA-IV), which facilitates the efficient BBB crossing of AAV variants 9P31 and 9P36 in mice 18 , 32 .\\nColocalization with Glut1 staining (magenta) highlights the transduction of endothelial cells.\\nFigure 1:\\n* Download figure\\n* Open in new tab\\nFigure 1: Human blood-brain barrier receptor-targeted AAV capsid engineering pipeline.\\nAfter narrowing down the pool of engineered AAVs specifically binding human CA-IV, single capsid variants were characterized in a “humanized” mouse model expressing human CA-IV in brain endothelial cells.\\nWe found that while the top performing capsid variants from receptor binding selections strongly bind human CA-IV and have enhanced potency on human CA-IV-expressing cells, they poorly transduced the “humanized” mouse brain after intravenous administration, with potencies akin to AAV9.\\nOne such variant, AAV-hCA4-IV77, resulted over 100-fold higher transgene mRNA levels compared to AAV9 and achieved widespread neuronal and astrocytic transduction (between 20–40% of neurons and 15–45% of astrocytes, depending on the brain region).\\nMoreover, AAV-hCA4-IV77 demonstrated approximately 2-fold greater overall cell transduction than AAV9 in human brain organoids, with a similar enhancement in mature neurons.\\nThese results suggest that tuning capsid receptor-binding strength may be required for optimal blood-brain barrier transcytosis and support the therapeutic potential of AAVs targeted to human CA-IV.\\nAmong human CA-IV-binding variants, AAV-hCA4-IV68 and AAV-hCA4-IV77 (hereafter abbreviated as IV68 and IV77) outperformed the previously identified strongly enriched and enhanced binders (Alpha 41, 48, 49, and 62), which had low Er and Eh scores in this context ( Figure 3A ).\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2025.04.21.649868v1\",\n      \"title\": \"AAVs Targeting Human Carbonic Anhydrase IV Enhance Gene Delivery to the Brain\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2025-04-22\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"AAVs Targeting Human Carbonic Anhydrase IV Enhance Gene Delivery to the Brain\\nHere, we engineer AAVs to target human carbonic anhydrase IV (CA-IV), a recently identified blood-brain barrier (BBB) transcytosis receptor. Among known transcytosis receptors, CA-IV is notable for its relatively specific expression in brain endothelial cells and the potency of AAVs that target it in mice.\\nNotably, we find that human CA-IV binding capsid variants that were poorly enriched in the pull-down selection outperform strong binders in vivo. The most promising vector, AAV-hCA4-IV77, engages human CA-IV to achieve 100-fold greater brain transduction than AAV9, with robust neuronal and astrocytic coverage throughout multiple brain regions.\\nOne such receptor is carbonic anhydrase IV (CA-IV), which facilitates the efficient BBB crossing of AAV variants 9P31 and 9P36 in mice18,32.\\nAfter narrowing down the pool of engineered AAVs specifically binding human CA-IV, single capsid variants were characterized in a “humanized” mouse model expressing human CA-IV in brain endothelial cells.\\nWe found that while the top performing capsid variants from receptor binding selections strongly bind human CA-IV and have enhanced potency on human CA-IV-expressing cells, they poorly transduced the “humanized” mouse brain after intravenous administration, with potencies akin to AAV9.\\nOne such variant, AAV-hCA4-IV77, resulted over 100-fold higher transgene mRNA levels compared to AAV9 and achieved widespread neuronal and astrocytic transduction (between 20–40% of neurons and 15–45% of astrocytes, depending on the brain region).\\nMoreover, AAV-hCA4-IV77 demonstrated approximately 2-fold greater overall cell transduction than AAV9 in human brain organoids, with a similar enhancement in mature neurons.\\nThese results suggest that tuning capsid receptor-binding strength may be required for optimal blood-brain barrier transcytosis and support the therapeutic potential of AAVs targeted to human CA-IV.\\nHowever, moderate binding affinity has been reported to enhance BBB transcytosis by antibodies^51.\\nTherefore, we utilized our “humanized” mouse model to perform an in vivo selection of human CA-IV-binding AAVs (Figure 3A).\\nAmong human CA-IV-binding variants, AAV-hCA4-IV68 and AAV-hCA4-IV77 (hereafter abbreviated as IV68 and IV77) outperformed the previously identified strongly enriched and enhanced binders (Alpha 41, 48, 49, and 62), which had low Er and Eh scores in this context (Figure 3A).\\nWhen produced as single clones, IV77 exhibited about a 5-fold lower production yield compared to AAV9 and addition of 300 mM NaCl to its buffer helped with stable storage, whereas AAV-hCA4-IV68 achieved yields comparable to AAV9 and required no such buffer formulation.\\nConsistent with their cell transduction selection rankings, whereas Alpha variants showed strong human CA-IV dependent HEK cell transduction (Figure 2C), IV68 and IV77 potency were not affected by human CA-IV expression (Figure S4A-B).\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.addgene.org/browse/article/28261434\",\n      \"title\": \"Addgene: AAVs targeting human carbonic anhydrase IV enhance gene delivery to the brain.\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Addgene Website Feedback\\nNeed help? Send us an email at help@addgene.org Privacy Policy\\n* Browse\\n* Viviana Gradinaru\\n* Lin et al\\nAAVs targeting human carbonic anhydrase IV enhance gene delivery to the brain.\\nLin C, Chen X, Hoang JD, Ristic F, Fan Y, Jang S, Chung JHA, Sullivan EE, Gawda T, Kavvathas B, Tran I, Li Y, Steele AD, Shay TF, Gradinaru V Cell Rep. 2025 Oct 31;44(11):116419. doi: 10.1016/j.celrep.2025.116419. (Link opens in a new window) PubMed (Link opens in a new window) Article\\nPlasmids from Article\\n* ID | Plasmid | Purpose\\n* 244906 | pUCmini-iCAP-hCA4-IV77 | Rep/Cap plasmid for producing variant AAV-hCA4-IV77\\n* 244907 | pUCmini-iCAP-hCA4-IV68 | Rep/Cap plasmid for producing variant AAV-hCA4-IV68\\n* 244908 | pAAV-CAG-hCA4-miR122 | AAV transgene plasmid encoding human CA4 with miR-122 target site\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2211124725011908\",\n      \"title\": \"Report AAVs targeting human carbonic anhydrase IV enhance gene ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Report AAVs targeting human carbonic anhydrase IV enhance gene ...\\nGene therapies using natural adeno-associated virus (AAV) serotypes have restricted applications, particularly in the brain, due to their poor targeting and\\nAAVs targeting human carbonic anhydrase IV enhance ...\\nby C Lin · 2025 · Cited by 7 — Viviana Gradinaru (viviana@caltech.edu ). AAV-PHP.B Administration Results in a Differential Pattern of CNS Biodistribution in Non-human\\nAAVs targeting human carbonic anhydrase IV enhance ...\\nby C Lin · 2025 · Cited by 7 — Here, we engineer AAVs to target human carbonic anhydrase IV (CA-IV), a recently identified blood-brain barrier transcytosis receptor with\\nAAVs targeting human carbonic anhydrase IV enhance ...\\nby C Lin · 2025 · Cited by 9 — Here, we engineer AAVs to target human carbonic anhydrase IV (CA-IV), a recently identified blood-brain barrier transcytosis receptor with several favorable\\nAAVs targeting human carbonic anhydrase IV enhance ...\\nby C Lin · 2025 · Cited by 8 — Both AAV-hCA4-IV68 and AAV-hCA4-IV77 demonstrate significantly enhanced and widespread transduction throughout the brains of “humanized” mice\\nAAVs targeting human carbonic anhydrase IV enhance ...\\nby C Lin · 2025 · Cited by 8 — The process of receptor-mediated BBB crossing by AAVs involves three key steps: receptor binding, cellular uptake, and transcytosis (Figure 1B).\\nAAVs targeting human carbonic anhydrase IV enhance ...\\nby C Lin · 2025 · Cited by 9 — AAV-hCA4-IV77, achieves 100-fold-greater brain transduction than AAV9, with robust neuronal and astrocytic coverage across brain regions.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/sciadv.adg6618\",\n      \"title\": \"Primate-conserved carbonic anhydrase IV and murine-restricted LY6C1 enable blood-brain barrier crossing by engineered viral vectors | Science Advances\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Screening potential cognate receptors for enhanced BBB crossing, we identify two targets: murine-restricted LY6C1 and widely conserved carbonic anhydrase IV (CA-IV).\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2023.01.12.523632v1\",\n      \"title\": \"Primate-conserved Carbonic Anhydrase IV and murine-restricted Ly6c1 are new targets for crossing the blood-brain barrier | bioRxiv\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Sullivan , View ORCID Profile Xiaozhe Ding , View ORCID Profile Xinhong Chen , View ORCID Profile Sripriya Ravindra Kumar , View ORCID Profile David Goertsen , David Brown , Jost Vielmetter , Máté Borsos , Annie W. Lam , View ORCID Profile Viviana Gradinaru doi: https://doi.org/10.1101/2023.01.12.523632 The blood-brain barrier (BBB) presents a major challenge to delivering large molecules to study and treat the central nervous system (CNS). This is due in part to the scarcity of effective targets for BBB crossing, the identification of which is the crucial first step of drug development. Here, we leveraged a panel of adeno-associated viruses (AAVs) previously identified through directed evolution for improved BBB transport to reverse engineer protein targets for enhanced BBB crossing. We identify both murine-restricted Ly6c1 and primate-conserved carbonic anhydrase IV (Car4; CA4) as novel receptors for crossing the BBB. We demonstrate how these receptors can unlock new experimental and computational target-focused engineering strategies by creating the enhanced Ly6c1-binding vector AAV-PHP.eC and by applying AlphaFold2-enabled in silico methods to rank capsids against identified receptors and generate capsid-receptor binding models.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"warnings\": null,\n  \"metadata\": null,\n  \"session_id\": \"ses_ea39878bfffeVgc2zisEYeoaLE\"\n}"
